From: Stained Glass OS <dev@stained-glass.example>
Subject: Seed a new per-user hive from userdef.reg, as Windows seeds a profile

Patch 0002 creates a per-user HKCU hive on demand for each uid, but creates it
*empty*. Windows does not: a new profile's hive is seeded from Default User.

Creating it empty looks harmless and is not. HKCU is where Wine keeps its own
per-user defaults, including the display and desktop settings under
Software\Wine\Explorer, so a user whose hive is empty gets none of them. The
symptom is not an error anywhere -- the shell starts, and draws itself for
Wine's built-in default screen size rather than the real one. On a 1280x800
desktop that is a 1024-wide, 4-pixel-high taskbar sitting at y=764, which
reads as a layout bug a long way from the registry.

userdef.reg is the hive Wine already loads as \Registry\User\.Default, and is
already present in every prefix, so it is the natural template. Only a hive
that does not exist yet is seeded; an existing one is loaded untouched.

Forwarded: not-needed
--- a/server/registry.c
+++ b/server/registry.c
@@ -1964,6 +1964,24 @@
      * string has to outlive the call. */
     if (asprintf( &filename, "user-%u.reg", (unsigned int)uid ) != -1)
     {
+        /* Seed a hive that does not exist yet from userdef.reg, the way Windows
+         * seeds a new profile from Default User.
+         *
+         * Creating it empty instead looks harmless and is not: HKCU is where
+         * Wine keeps its own per-user defaults, so a user with an empty hive
+         * gets none of them. The visible result is not an error -- the shell
+         * comes up believing the screen is Wine's built-in default size, and
+         * draws a taskbar to match on a display of some other size. */
+        if (access( filename, F_OK ) != 0)
+        {
+            FILE *f;
+            if ((f = fopen( "userdef.reg", "r" )))
+            {
+                load_keys( key, "userdef.reg", f, 0 );
+                fclose( f );
+                clear_error();
+            }
+        }
         load_init_registry_from_file( filename, key );
         sg_loaded_uids[sg_loaded_uid_count++] = uid;
     }
